e 13 recognized species found worldwide (except in Australia and Antarctica)"},"groundingChunkIndices": [1,2]},{"segment": {"startIndex": 222,"endIndex": 290,"text": "They inhabit both freshwater (rivers, lakes) and marine environments"},"groundingChunkIndices": [0]},{"segment": {"startIndex": 292,"endIndex": 446,"text": "* **Physical Adaptations:** Built for swimming, they have streamlined bodies, webbed paws, muscular tails, and ears and nostrils that seal shut underwater"},"groundingChunkIndices": [0,1]},{"segment": {"startIndex": 448,"endIndex": 615,"text": "Unlike most marine mammals, they lack blubber; instead, they stay warm with exceptionally dense, water-repellent fur—sea otters possess the densest fur of any animal"},"groundingChunkIndices": [0,1,3]},{"segment": {"startIndex": 617,"endIndex": 721,"text": "* **Diet:** Carnivores that primarily feed on fish, crabs, shellfish, frogs, and small aquatic creatures"},"groundingChunkIndices": [0]},{"segment": {"startIndex": 723,"endIndex": 798,"text": "* **Behavior:** Known for high intelligence, playfulness, and social nature"},"groundingChunkIndices": [0,2]},{"segment": {"startIndex": 800,"endIndex": 949,"text": "Sea otters are among the few animals known to use tools (cracking open shells using rocks) and often hold paws while floating to avoid drifting apart"},"groundingChunkIndices": [4,2]},{"segment": {"startIndex": 951,"endIndex": 1067,"text": "* **Conservation:** Many species are currently threatened or endangered due to habitat loss, pollution, and poaching"},"groundingChunkIndices": [2,5]}],"webSearchQueries": ["otters facts summary"]}}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 12,"candidatesTokenCount": 339,"totalTokenCount": 351,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 12}],"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-3.8-flash","responseId": "Vvy6av6cOJ6A1e8P5p2cmQ8"}

